# Working on [[.Name]]

This file is for anyone changing this project, people and coding agents alike. [[.Name]] is a [kakarot](https://github.com/gsoultan/kakarot) service.

## Commands

```console
$ go generate ./...        # after changing service/service.go or data/model
$ go test ./...            # usecase and HTTP tests need no database
$ go tool kakarot check    # layer rules, //kakarot: comments, generated code is current
$ go vet ./... && gofmt -l .
```

A change is done when all four pass.

## Layers

Each package is one layer. A layer imports only the layers below it.

| Package | Holds | May import |
|---|---|---|
| `cmd/server` | `main`: wiring by hand, no DI container | everything |
| `transport/` | generated HTTP routes; tests of the API | `endpoint`, `domain` |
| `endpoint/` | generated typed endpoints | `service`, `domain` |
| `service/` | the API as a Go interface with `//kakarot:` comments | `usecase`, `domain` |
| `usecase/` | business rules; ports (interfaces) for what they need | `domain` |
| `domain/` | request, response and event types, validation | nothing in this project |
| `data/` | storm repositories that implement the ports | `usecase`, `domain` |

`go tool kakarot check` enforces this table.

## Rules

- Never edit `*_gen.go`, `*.gen.go` or anything in `data/store`. Change the source and run `go generate ./...`.
- `usecase`, `service` and `domain` never import `net/http`, gRPC, storm or `data`. A usecase that needs the outside world declares an interface in `usecase` and `data` implements it.
- Domain types are the DTOs: every layer above `data` speaks them. The types in `data/model` are database types and never leave `data`.
- Return errors with `kakarot.E`, `kakarot.NotFound`, `kakarot.Invalid` or `kakarot.Conflict`. Transports map the code to a status. Compare with `kakarot.Is(err, code)`.
- A request type with a `Validate() error` method is validated on every transport automatically.
- Work that must happen together goes in one `Tx.Within(ctx, ...)` in the usecase. Repositories take the transaction from `ctx` with `data.Executor(ctx, db)`.

## Add an operation

1. Add the request and response types to `domain/`, with `Validate` if the request has rules.
2. Write a failing test in `usecase/` with an in-memory port, then the usecase.
3. Add the method to the interface in `service/service.go` with its comment, and implement it by calling the usecase:

   ```go
   //kakarot:http DELETE /notes/{id}
   DeleteNote(ctx context.Context, in domain.DeleteNote) (domain.Deleted, error)
   ```

4. Add the usecase to `service.Usecases` and wire it in `cmd/server/main.go`.
5. If it needs storage, add the method to the port and implement it in `data/`.
6. Run the four commands above.

Method shapes and comments:

| Shape | Kind |
|---|---|
| `(ctx, Req) (Resp, error)` | request and response |
| `(ctx, Req) iter.Seq2[Msg, error]` | server stream (SSE or NDJSON) |
| `(ctx, iter.Seq2[Msg, error]) (Resp, error)` | client stream (NDJSON upload) |
| `(ctx, iter.Seq2[In, error], func(Out) error) error` | two-way stream (WebSocket) |

| Comment | Effect |
|---|---|
| `//kakarot:http METHOD /path/{id}` | HTTP route; `path:"id"`, `query:"q"` and `header:"X"` tags fill the request |
| `//kakarot:grpc` | gRPC method |
| `//kakarot:queue topic.name` | message handler for the topic |

## Tests

- Usecases: in-memory ports, call `Execute`. Use `kakarottest.Tx` when the usecase opens a transaction.
- API: build the router with in-memory ports and call it with `kakarottest.Do`, as in `transport/http_test.go`.
- Asynchronous results: `kakarottest.Eventually`. Time: pass `kakarottest.NewClock(...).Now`.
- Repositories: test against a real PostgreSQL, skipped when no DSN is set.
